content:
gabi: Heads-up: found a parity bug between our Java and Kotlin SDKs around timeouts -> retry behavior on Android 11/12 devices. Repro in sample app with network shaping.

henry: can you summarize symptoms? hangs, dupes, crashes?

gabi: Mostly duplicate POSTs and a handful of "stuck" requests that never finish. Repro steps: call a POST that uploads a small JSON payload, induce network slowness via `adb shell network delay tcp:2000` and then trigger Activity.onPause which cancels the coroutine scope. Sometimes server receives two POSTs, sometimes the client never returns.

nora: which OkHttp timeouts are you using? callTimeout/readTimeout/connectTimeout? and okhttp version?

gabi: Default SDK OkHttp except we set callTimeout(30s) and connectTimeout(5s). OkHttp 4.11.0 on Pixel 8 (Android 14) and 4.10.0 on emulators. Behavior diverged after a client bump.

henry: important detail: callTimeout cancels the whole Call by throwing InterruptedIOException; readTimeout can also interrupt streams. If you do manual retries after catching IOException you might re-send a non-idempotent POST. Are we doing clone().execute() anywhere?

gabi: yes our legacy helper does something like: ```kotlin
suspend fun safeExecute(call: Call): Response = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
  try { return@withContext call.execute() }
  catch (e: IOException) { // network glitch -> retry once
    return@withContext call.clone().execute()
  }
}
```

qa-jo: that pattern is risky with coroutines. clone() creates an independent Call that doesn't inherit cancellation. If the coroutine was cancelled during the first execute, you'll still run the clone and send again.

nora: also note OkHttp changed some timeout handling between 4.10 and 4.11; timeouts may now manifest as InterruptedIOException earlier in the stack which interacts badly with cancellation handling.

gabi: we also have an AuthInterceptor that does synchronous refresh inside intercept(), and in some traces we saw interleaving: refresh call blocks while original call times out -> retry helper replays -> server receives two.

henry: two-fold mitigation: 1) stop retrying non-idempotent methods unless caller provided an Idempotency-Key header, 2) switch to a cancellable suspend wrapper around Call that uses enqueue + suspendCancellableCoroutine so cancellation cancels the active call instead of racing.

nora: sample cancellable wrapper:
```kotlin
suspend fun Call.awaitResponse(): Response = suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->
  enqueue(object: Callback {
    override fun onResponse(c: Call, r: Response) { if (!cont.isCancelled) cont.resume(r) }
    override fun onFailure(c: Call, e: IOException) { if (!cont.isCancelled) cont.resumeWithException(e) }
  })
  cont.invokeOnCancellation { cancel() }
}
```

qa-jo: also propose adding a small CI emulator job that runs the sample app with varied network delays and cancels coroutines to assert no duplicate POSTs. Can run 1000 iterations and fail the build if server saw >1 request for the same id.

gabi: agreed. I'll: 1) remove naive clone() retry from SDK core, 2) add idempotency guard + telemetry event when a retry is attempted, 3) replace execute-based helper with awaitResponse wrapper, and 4) add emulator stress test in CI.

henry: for auth refresh, isolate refresh requests onto a separate OkHttpClient without auth interceptors to avoid recursion and accidental double-submits. Also document in migration notes for customers using interceptor-based refresh.

infra-bot: created ticket SDK-4420: "Android SDK: replace execute() retry helper with cancellable enqueue wrapper + CI emulator regression" and queued emulator job.

qa-jo: I'll run a targeted smoke on Pixel 8 farm once the QA build lands and post numbers here. If flaky, we may need a hotfix.

gabi: thanks everyone. PR incoming with tests and a small sample app that reproduces the issue locally. Will add a changelog note about retry semantics change. :thumbsup:
